The {{nihongo|'''Hitotsubashi Line'''|一橋線|Hitotsubashi-sen}} is a [[rapid transit]] line operated by [[Okami Metro Co., Ltd.]] in [[Okami]], [[Okaiken]]. Designated as '''Line 11''' of the Okami Metro network, it is identified by the color '''crimson''' ({{HTML color|#dc143c}}) and carries the line code '''H'''. The line runs from {{nihongo|Kifune|貴船}} in the northeast to {{nihongo|Takahama|高浜}} in the southwest, spanning {{cvt|52.7|km|mi|0}} and serving 36 stations. It is one of the longest and highest-capacity lines in the Okami Metro system.

The Hitotsubashi Line is a major cross-city corridor, connecting the suburban districts of Hojo and Yodo in the northeast with the central wards of Nagata, Aoi, and Sugamo, before continuing southwest through the Mitsu-Minato ward to the coastal district of Takahama. The line's name, meaning "single bridge," references a historic bridge that once spanned the Tedori River at the point where the line's central section now runs.

The line operates '''Type B''' rolling stock, consisting of 8-car trains approximately {{cvt|160|m|ft|0}} in length with a car width of {{cvt|2.8|m|ft|0}}. This gives the Hitotsubashi Line a higher passenger capacity than the 6-car Type C lines, making it one of the network's key high-volume routes. The line offers interchanges with 13 other metro lines, including major transfer hubs at [[Aoi (Gakushuin-mae) (H)|Aoi (Gakushuin-mae)]] (with the [[Miyoshi Line]]), Hino (with the [[Nanboku Line (Okami)|Nanboku Line]]), and Hamada-machi (with the [[Tedorigawa Line]]).

==History==

===Planning and construction===

The Hitotsubashi Line was conceived in the late 1970s as part of the fifth phase of expansion of the Okami Municipal Subway. By this time, the city's rapid growth had pushed residential development far into the northeastern suburbs of Hojo and Yodo, as well as the southwestern coastal district of Takahama. The existing metro lines did not adequately serve these areas, and commuters were forced to rely on buses and private railways, leading to severe congestion on surface routes.

The line was designed to serve as a high-capacity cross-city corridor, connecting the northeastern and southwestern suburbs through the city center. The alignment was chosen to pass through several densely populated wards that lacked direct metro access, including Yanagimoto and the growing Mitsu-Minato waterfront area. The decision to use 8-car Type B rolling stock was made to accommodate the high projected ridership, particularly during peak commuting hours.

Construction began in 1982. The project was one of the most complex in the Okami Metro's history, as the line's route required deep-bore tunneling through the hilly terrain of the Hojo and Terao districts, as well as a crossing of the Tedori River in the central section. The river crossing was accomplished using a shield tunnel method, which was then a relatively new technology in Okaiken.

===Opening and early operation===

The first segment of the Hitotsubashi Line opened on September 10, 1988, between Kifune and Aoi (Gakushuin-mae), covering approximately 25 kilometers and 18 stations. The opening was a major event for the northeastern suburbs, which had previously been served only by buses. The line quickly attracted high ridership, with daily passenger counts exceeding 400,000 within the first year.

The second phase of construction extended the line southwest from Aoi through the Sugamo and Mitsu-Minato wards to Takahama, completing the full route in 1993. This extension added 18 new stations and brought the total line length to {{cvt|52.7|km|mi|0}}. The extension through the Mitsu-Minato ward was particularly important, as it provided a direct metro connection to the port area and the newly developed Takahama coastal residential district.

===Modernization===

In the 2000s, the Hitotsubashi Line underwent a program of station modernization, with older stations receiving escalator and elevator installations, improved signage, and platform screen doors at the busiest stations. The line was one of the first in the network to receive platform screen doors, as part of a safety initiative following a series of incidents on the network.

Following the privatization of the [[Okami Municipal Transportation Bureau]] in 2018, the Hitotsubashi Line received new 5000 series rolling stock manufactured by [[Okami Heavy Industries]]. The 5000 series, introduced in 2018, is shared with the [[Miyoshi Line]], [[Asahi Line]], [[Nanboku Line (Okami)|Nanboku Line]], and [[Nagata Line]]. The line also continues to operate a small number of legacy 2000 series trains, which are being progressively retired as additional 5000 series sets are delivered.

