==Rolling stock==

The Hitotsubashi Line operates '''Type B''' rolling stock, consisting of 8-car trains approximately {{cvt|160|m|ft|0}} in length with a car width of {{cvt|2.8|m|ft|0}}. This provides significantly higher capacity than the 6-car Type C lines, making the Hitotsubashi Line one of the network's key high-volume routes.

;Current fleet
* {{nihongo|'''5000 series'''|5000系}} — Introduced in 2018, manufactured by [[Okami Heavy Industries]]. These trains form the current backbone of the Hitotsubashi Line fleet, featuring modern LED lighting, accessibility improvements, and energy-efficient propulsion systems. The 5000 series is shared with the [[Miyoshi Line]], [[Asahi Line]], [[Nanboku Line (Okami)|Nanboku Line]], and [[Nagata Line]].

;Legacy fleet
* {{nihongo|'''2000 series'''|2000系}} — Originally introduced in 1981 for the [[Shitayanagi Line]] and [[Tedorigawa Line]], a small number of 2000 series sets were reassigned to the Hitotsubashi Line in the 1990s to supplement capacity. These trains are being progressively retired as additional 5000 series sets are delivered. Full retirement is expected by 2027.

==Through-service==

The Hitotsubashi Line does not currently operate through-service with any other railway operator. The line's standard gauge alignment makes it technically compatible with other Okami Metro lines, and there have been periodic proposals to introduce through-running with the [[Miyoshi Line]] at Aoi (Gakushuin-mae), as both lines use Type B rolling stock. As of 2026, no such through-service has been implemented.

The line's extensive interchange network, with 13 transfer hubs, provides passengers with convenient connections to nearly every other line in the Okami Metro system. The interchange at Aoi (Gakushuin-mae) with the [[Miyoshi Line]] is one of the busiest in the network, as it connects two of the system's highest-capacity lines.

